 "eras": [
  {"id":"1250","year":"c. 1250","label":"Hospitaller concentric castle","short":"c. 1250","lens":"none","summary":"The Hospitaller castle after its final construction phase: a low outer enceinte with round towers around a higher inner enceinte, the three-tower keep at the south, the great Gothic hall, stables and chapel.","basis":"KRAK-MIN: the castle built from after 1160, rebuilt after the earthquakes of 1202-03 with a double enceinte, the great hall and stables in the mid-13th century. The documented layer is only the two OSM lines carried back; every tower, hall and gate is on the constructed layer.","conjecture":"No plan was read, so no tower, gate or building is placed from a survey: positions follow the OSM lines and are assumed (+-15 m at best). The OSM outline includes Mamluk fabric (Qalawun's tower of 1285) and cannot be separated from the 13th-century line. Not drawn: the great cistern in the south ditch, the entrance ramp and its three turns, the keep's interior, the glacis, the town and the lists' extent.","sources":["KRAK-MIN","KRAK-ARAMCO","KRAK-WHE"],"facts":["KRAK-MIN: oldest remains date from after 1160; the first castle had an irregular polygonal enceinte, a main east gate with two quadrangular towers, a vaulted gallery against its inner face, a chapel on the east face, a great tower (G) to the south and a latrine tower (D) to the north-west.","KRAK-MIN: new fortifications began in 1202-03 after earthquakes; the keep is three round towers linked by buildings; the final phase added the outer enceinte with round bastions, the great hall (27 x 7.5 m) and the 60 m stables, and made the old southern ditch a large cistern.","KRAK-MIN: about 60 knights were in garrison, each with a sergeant, and a theoretical 300 horses; KRAK-ARAMCO gives an 80 ft (about 24 m) thick south wall."]},
  {"id":"1271","year":"1271","label":"Baybars' siege","short":"1271","lens":"none","summary":"The castle as Sultan Baybars besieged it from February to 8 April 1271: the Hospitaller layout, before the south-west tower fell and the Mamluk refortification.","basis":"KRAK-MIN (Baybars' siege): Baybars' army took the town, then the \"blacksmith's\" barbican; Aleppo miners undermined towers on the south, north and east; a tower collapsed on 30 March and the lower ward was entered; the garrison asked for aman on 8 April.","conjecture":"The constructed geometry is the same as c. 1250: the page does not name the tower that collapsed or its face, so no breach is drawn. The blacksmith's barbican is not located and not drawn. The southern face \"took the heaviest projectile damage\" and was refaced, but how much stood before is unknown.","sources":["KRAK-MIN","KRAK-WHE"],"facts":["KRAK-MIN: miners dug under towers on the south, north and east faces; one collapsed on 30 March; the southern face was completely refaced afterwards; the northern postern (Nicolas Lorgne's barbican) and the east gate sector were badly damaged and rebuilt.","KRAK-WHE: Baybars arrived on 21 February, attacked from 3 March, used catapults from 18 March; the Hospitallers surrendered on 8 April, possibly after a forged letter; they left for Cyprus by 1291."]},
  {"id":"today","year":"Today","label":"Mamluk castle and monument","short":"Monument","lens":"none","summary":"The castle as it stands after the Mamluk rebuilding: the south-west tower of 1271, the south-east gate tower, Qalawun's south tower of 1285, the east barlong towers, the hammam and the chapel turned into a mosque.","basis":"KRAK-MIN (Mamluk refortification, Baybars' alterations, Qalawun's tower, entrance system, hammam): the Mamluks kept the Hospitaller plan, refaced and raised it with machicolated crowns, and added the towers above. The documented layer is the OSM lines.","conjecture":"No dated plan or photograph of the present state was read, and the damage of the Syrian civil war (2011-2014), reported only as limited by KRAK-WHE, is not shown. Heights are assumed. The positions of the east gate tower, barlong towers and south-east tower are assumptions on the OSM east line. The Mamluk palace over the hall's portico (only a portal survives) and the vaulted ramp are not drawn.","sources":["KRAK-MIN","KRAK-ARAMCO","KRAK-WHE"],"facts":["KRAK-MIN: Qalawun's tower is dated 684 AH (1285); the east gate tower carries inscriptions of 1301-02 and 1345; the south-east gate tower carries inscriptions of 1271 and 1278; the south-west tower was rebuilt about 15 m in diameter.","KRAK-MIN: the Mamluks converted the chapel into a mosque (three mihrabs in the south wall, a minbar) and built a hammam in the south-east lists.","KRAK-WHE: UNESCO-listed; the Syrian civil war damage is described as limited."]}
 ],
